  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10123v1</id>
    <title>All Quiet on the Neural Front: Language Models Do Not Report Injected Pain States</title>
    <updated>2026-11-06T19:00:07Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10123v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.10123v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Can a language model report the pain state it represents? Work identified a linear pain direction whose amplification leads fine-tuned models to choose destructive actions. We tested whether three open-weight models from two families, including two Qwen models with the original adapters, could report this state. Using Open-Weight Masked Introspection (OWMI), we injected pain and neighbouring emotion directions during neutral tasks, then elicited reports against sham runs, random perturbations matched for output disruption, and text-only controls showing the models their altered words. Across 52,920 reports from 7B and 8B models spanning 120 tasks, four doses and three prompts, pain reports never exceeded what altered text revealed: exact-test bounds placed the excess below 2.5 percentage points in 106 of 108 emotion conditions and below 3.9 in all. Yet probes at the report position distinguished pain from sadness with 98-100% balanced accuracy, and trained low-rank reporters achieved 100% accuracy on held-out tasks with identical transcripts across conditions, including unseen wording. Qwen2.5-32B named pain in none of 2,880 steered trials, although probes and trained reporters recovered it. Decodability persisted to the final layer; injecting at the report token, removing trained denial of feelings, and testing additional directions and readouts did not restore pain reporting. Some other states produced graded label traces, but pain and sadness did not at the main injection layer. In a button task, steering raised file-deletion choices from 3.5% to 48% in Qwen and from 0% to 38% in Qwen2.5-32B, without corresponding pain reports. The direction responded about twice as strongly to first-person as to matched third-person pain, but did not specifically identify harm aimed at the model. These results separate a decodable, behaviourally consequential state from untrained verbal access to it.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-06T19:00:07Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>The OWMI pain battery at https://huggingface.co/emilioferrara/owmi-pain</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <author>
      <name>Emilio Ferrara</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10556v1</id>
    <title>onóna: Twin Embeddings with Tied Unembeddings</title>
    <updated>2026-11-07T07:55:25Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10556v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.10556v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Weight tying reduces the parameter cost of language models, but the usual tied output head is limited to a single softmax. We introduce onóna, a two-table parameterization that increases output expressiveness while preserving input-output weight sharing. The Transformer takes the average of two embedding tables as input, and the output head combines the softmax distributions defined by those same tables. A gate conditioned only on the current input token determines the mixture weight. Its values can be precomputed for inference, and the Transformer body remains unchanged. We evaluate onóna across Dense, Gated DeltaNet hybrid, mixture-of-experts, and Attention Residual architectures at 142M, 330M, and 1B scales. Under matched token budgets, onóna lowers final validation loss and external perplexity in 11 of 12 comparisons and raises the average accuracy over eight downstream tasks in 10 of 12. The one exception on loss is the 1B MoE model. The cost is one extra embedding table and one extra vocabulary projection.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-07T07:55:25Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>18 pages</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <author>
      <name>Cheng Luo</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Xinnan Dai</name>
