  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10486v1</id>
    <title>Test-Time Scaling for Diffusion Language Models with Frequency-Aware Remasking</title>
    <updated>2026-11-07T05:26:09Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10486v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.10486v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Diffusion language models (DLMs) generate text through iterative denoising, where decisions about which tokens to commit or remask strongly affect output quality. Existing certainty-aware strategies rely on instantaneous confidence or entropy, but these local signals can be misleading on difficult reasoning problems. We formulate DLM test-time scaling as adaptive allocation of both inference compute and decoding strategies. Our two-stage approach uses a fast base sampler to solve easy questions, then applies frequency-aware remasking to the remaining hard questions. This strategy tracks how often each token's prediction changes across denoising steps and preferentially commits frequently changing tokens, encouraging samples to explore alternative reasoning paths rather than collapse onto the model's preferred trajectory. Experiments with LLaDA-8B-Instruct and Dream-7B-Instruct show consistent improvements over a strong adaptive allocation baseline, with absolute accuracy gains of up to 3.73 percentage points (LLaDA) and 7.33 percentage points (Dream) on MATH-500, and absolute coverage gains of up to 11.39 percentage points (LLaDA) and 10.98 percentage points (Dream) on HumanEval. On questions unsolved by the base certainty-aware strategy after 100 samples, frequency-aware remasking recovers solutions for 3.75% to 18.75% of examples.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-07T05:26:09Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <author>
      <name>Bowen Zuo</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yue Yu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Dongruo Zhou</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yinglun Zhu</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03142v1</id>
    <title>DepthGraft: Structural Regularization through Hierarchical Cross-Layer KV Reconstruction</title>
    <updated>2026-11-02T13:52:03Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03142v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.03142v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Scaling depth is a key route to stronger large language models, but Pre-LN architectures often suffer from depth-wise information dilution, limiting the effective use of deep layers. While cross-layer connectivity has proven effective at mitigating this problem, existing designs usually introduce additional computation or memory access. We revisit cross-layer KV sharing as an efficient form of such connectivity. Beyond cache compression, each KV reuse edge creates a direct gradient path from deep layers to source KV projections, turning KV sharing into an implicit structural regularizer for KV representation geometry. We provide a systematic analysis of this mechanism, showing that proper sharing topologies sharpen key retrieval directions while enriching the value content subspace, whereas existing adjacent or global-anchor designs do not fully exploit this effect. Motivated by this, we propose DepthGraft, a hierarchical KV-sharing architecture that reconstructs deep-layer KV from stride-aligned shallow-middle source pairs. DepthGraft distributes non-local gradient flow across source layers, while preserving prefill early completion and reducing KV cache by roughly one third. Experiments on dense and MoE models from 0.5B to 16B parameters show consistent downstream improvements under both GQA and MLA. Notably, on the 16B MoE model, DepthGraft achieves a 7.0% absolute improvement on MMLU-Pro, along with gains of 6.6% on CMMLU and 6.4% on C3. These results validate the potential of DepthGraft as a scalable design for large-scale pretraining. The project page is available at https://github.com/jianke0604/DepthGraft.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-02T13:52:03Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <author>
      <name>Xiaohan Qin</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Xiangdong Zhang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yu Wang</name>
    </author>
