  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10304v1</id>
    <title>Longtail-SWE: High-Quality SWE Tasks for Underrepresented Languages</title>
    <updated>2026-11-06T23:04:32Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10304v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.10304v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Challenging repository-level benchmarks remain limited for many programming language ecosystems, where filtering scarce candidates with ambiguous descriptions or defective tests can discard valuable engineering problems. We introduce Longtail-SWE, a benchmark of 202 issue-resolution tasks across 13 languages with limited coverage in existing repository-level benchmarks: Clojure, Dart, Elixir, Erlang, F#, Haskell, Julia, Kotlin, Lua, OCaml, R, Scala, and Swift. Its construction uses a solver--judge--repairer pipeline: a solver attempts each candidate task, and a judge reviews the task together with the solver's trajectory and test results to guide revisions to tests and task descriptions. These revisions aim to correct tests that reject valid alternative implementations or accept incorrect solutions while retaining challenging tasks and preserving their intended requirements. We evaluate nine model configurations, including frontier models, and analyze task-solving performance, consistency across repeated attempts, and resource usage. We release all benchmark instances, executable environments, complete construction and evaluation tooling, and agent trajectories, together with an additional set of easier tasks to support evaluation of smaller models.</summary>
    <category term="cs.SE" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-06T23:04:32Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.SE"/>
    <author>
      <name>Vladimir Dobrovolskii</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Ibragim Badertdinov</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Alexander Golubev</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11344v1</id>
    <title>Spatial Harness: Grounding Spatial Reasoning in Executable World States with Physics Engines</title>
    <updated>2026-11-08T12:43:14Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11344v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.11344v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Spatial reasoning requires understanding how objects and observers are arranged in a scene and how their relations change with viewpoint or movement. Vision-language models (VLMs) typically perform both steps through model-internal reasoning, forming an implicit scene interpretation from partial views and imagining the consequences of spatial transformations. A misread scene can lead to a wrong answer, while even a correct interpretation does not ensure that imagined transformations satisfy spatial constraints. We introduce Spatial Harness, which represents scenes as explicit and executable world states to ground spatial reasoning in physics-engine feedback. A Perceiver integrates scene constraints extracted from the question with specialized visual estimates to place objects and cameras in a shared coordinate frame. The resulting state is instantiated in a physics engine, where a Reasoner executes viewpoint changes and uses computed measurements and rendered views to guide reasoning. We evaluate on MindCube, SAT, MMSI-Bench, and SpatialRGPT-Bench with open- and closed-weight VLMs. On MindCube, Spatial Harness improves Qwen3.5-27B from 52.70% to 71.14%, surpassing pySpatial by 7.52 percentage points. Given identical annotated object and camera poses, it reaches 98.25% versus 71.81% for model-internal reasoning. Code is publicly available at: https://github.com/tmlr-group/Spatial-Harness</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-08T12:43:14Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>NeurIPS 2026</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <author>
      <name>Chentao Cao</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Zhanke Zhou</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Sangni Duan</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Bo Han</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Hang Li</name>
    </author>
