  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12875v1</id>
    <title>Click3R: Interactive Stereo 3D Reconstruction with Sparse Correspondence Clicks</title>
    <updated>2026-11-09T18:58:02Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12875v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.12875v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Despite impressive progress, existing feed-forward large 3D reconstruction models produce a single, irrevocable prediction. When these models fail, there is no mechanism to correct their mistakes. To address this limitation, we introduce Click3R, a framework for interactive geometric correction of feed-forward models using sparse human correspondence clicks. Given an image pair and a small set of cross-view point correspondences provided by a user, Click3R injects geometric constraints into the 3D reconstruction networks through a lightweight point correspondence adapter, enabling the model to resolve ambiguities in challenging scenarios such as repetitive textures, symmetric structures, large viewpoint changes, or textureless regions. To evaluate interactive reconstruction, we curate a challenging benchmark specifically designed to expose failure modes of existing feed-forward 3D reconstruction methods. Experiments show that Click3R reduces reconstruction error with as few as a single correspondence click, improving performance on both standard and our curated benchmarks. Our results highlight that sparse human interaction provides an effective and practical mechanism for correcting geometric reconstruction errors.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-09T18:58:02Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>NeurIPS 2026. Project page: https://uva-computer-vision-lab.github.io/click3r/</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>Hao Gu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Jin Yao</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Zezhou Cheng</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11450v1</id>
    <title>How Much Training-Inference Mismatch Can On-Policy Distillation Tolerate?</title>
    <updated>2026-11-08T15:22:47Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11450v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.11450v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>On-policy distillation (OPD) trains a student language model on its own responses, guided by a fixed teacher. Generating these responses is expensive, so systems use quantized inference and asynchronous rollouts to reduce the cost. Both, however, introduce training-inference mismatch between the rollout engine and the model being trained. Such mismatch can destabilize reinforcement learning (RL), but its effects on OPD remain unclear. This paper investigates how much mismatch OPD tolerates, where its effects arise, and how training systems can use this tolerance. Measuring all mismatch sources on one scale, we examine quantization across three student-teacher pairs, with additional tests of weight staleness and injected noise on one pair. OPD tolerates substantial mismatch without meaningful loss of training quality but degrades at higher levels, revealing an empirical mismatch budget. Both the quality cost of mismatch and the mismatch introduced by quantization depend on the model, with some layers particularly sensitive to reduced precision. In controlled comparisons, OPD remains stable under rollout configurations that destabilize RL methods. Under weight noise, mismatch also shifts the prefixes reached during generation, introducing errors that per-token corrections cannot address. To put these findings into practice, we introduce a lightweight admission check that screens rollout configurations against a calibrated budget without requiring a full training run for each candidate. Model-specific FP8 recipes preserve training quality while providing capacity for at least $1.93\times$ as many KV-cache tokens as BF16. Combining FP8 with asynchronous rollouts reaches the same training quality $1.62\times$ sooner than synchronous BF16 training. Code is available at https://github.com/benyucong/train-inference-mismatch-opd</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-08T15:22:47Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>28 pages, 10 figures, 12 tables</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <author>
      <name>Cong Yu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Ziting Wang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Songlin Jiang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Sitong Zhang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Chengliang Chai</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Youhe Jiang</name>
