  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13885v1</id>
    <title>Are Causal Neuron Circuits Unique and Transferable?</title>
    <updated>2026-11-10T10:47:06Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13885v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.13885v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Interpretability demonstrations often show that intervening on a group of neurons changes a model's answer. A recent example removes an 18-neuron group in Qwen3-32B and the model answers "Simba" instead of "Scar" to a two-hop Lion King riddle. Such a demonstration establishes that the group matters, but not how much of it is necessary, whether other groups could do the same, or whether the effect carries over to other inputs. We ask: when intervening on a group of neurons changes a model's answer, how much of that group is actually necessary? For every selected group we measure all 4,096 subsets of 12 MLP neurons. On a two-alternative capital task (Qwen3-0.6B), zero-ablating 12 selected neurons lowers the answer margin by 27% while random neurons barely change it, yet replacing the same neurons with their values from other questions also barely changes it: their activations are nearly constant across questions, and we find no evidence that they carry question-specific content. On two-hop relation chains, patching 12 neurons with activations from the first-hop question shifts the answer to the bridge entity (from Scar to Simba): the greedy answer starts with the bridge in 9/11 chains for Qwen3-1.7B, 9/11 for Llama-3.2-1B and 8/10 for Gemma-3-1B; random neurons never do. The full group is rarely necessary: a few neurons often suffice, and in many chains disjoint subsets each flip the answer on their own, in an exploratory analysis most often when the model is less confident. Group effects transfer to rephrasings of the same chain but are much weaker on other tested chains. We study neuron-level causal interventions and alternative subsets; we do not recover the full connectivity of circuits.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-10T10:47:06Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>12 pages, 5 figures, 4 tables. Code, data and results: https://github.com/omtekyav/causal-neuron-circuits</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <author>
      <name>Ömer Tekin Yavuz</name>
      <arxiv:affiliation>İletişim Yazılım</arxiv:affiliation>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13028v1</id>
    <title>GTAGym: Real AAA Game Tasks Environment for Evaluation and Reinforcement Learning</title>
    <updated>2026-11-09T08:59:36Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13028v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.13028v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>AAA games offer a rich testbed for evaluating agent perception, reasoning, and control. However, evaluating whether agents can complete tasks in AAA games is challenging and often human-annotated because task outcomes are often difficult to verify automatically, let alone applying it for Reinforcement Learning (RL). We introduce GTAGym, an environment and framework for evaluation and RL on GTA V tasks. Its evaluation benchmark, GTAVBench, derived from GTA V main-story missions, contains 103 online tasks reconstructed across nine task families, alongside 47 image-based shooting and sniping tasks using GTA V imagery. A web server provides access to the online environment and evaluation interface, supporting parallel testing through screenshots and keyboard-mouse actions with automatic engine-state outcome checks. We evaluate six frontier LLMs on GTAVBench with textual task instructions and on its GTAVBench-AGI branch, where instructions appear only in the first screenshot and a harness automatically compresses interaction history. The best configuration achieves 70.9% overall accuracy in GTAVBench-AGI, yet aerial navigation, evasion and interception remain difficult. GTAGym also provides a scalable generator that samples task parameters within user-specified ranges and option sets under fixed templates, creating batches of 3D and image-based shooting environments for RL with automatic reward and termination signals. LoRA RL of Qwen3.8-27B on generated on-foot navigation tasks improves the in-domain performance and transfers to out-of-domain task categories, and native GTA V tasks from 15% to 23%.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-09T08:59:36Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>Jiajun Chen</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Shicheng Fang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Jiahao Lu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yuxin Wang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Xipeng Qiu</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13572v1</id>
    <title>Market-Conditioned JEPA: Learning from Unpredictable Market Shocks</title>
    <updated>2026-11-10T04:40:04Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13572v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.13572v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Much of the variation in financial time series is driven by market-wide shocks that cannot be predicted from an asset's own history. Per-asset self-supervised learning treats this variation as irreducible noise. Under a latent factor model, we show that conditioning prediction on the realized market factor makes the asset's market exposure necessary for optimal prediction, while reducing the noise against which the asset-specific component is learned. Building on this insight, we introduce Market-Conditioned JEPA (MC-JEPA), a joint-embedding predictive architecture for financial time series. During training, we construct a leave-one-out market condition for each asset from the future target embeddings of other assets and incorporate it through factor loadings derived from the asset's own representation. On 11 years of minute-level Chinese A-share data, MC-JEPA improves over per-asset JEPA across nine targets spanning returns, market exposure, risk, and liquidity, with the strongest and most statistically significant gains in exposure, risk, and liquidity. It achieves the best RankIC on all liquidity targets and on return horizons from two hours to five days, outperforming time-series foundation models and self-supervised baselines, and delivers the best portfolio performance in a historical backtest. Ablations show that the return and exposure gains require the condition to be aligned with the realized market movement. After restoring the volatility scale removed by normalization, the predictor's factor loadings rank stocks by market beta nearly as well as a supervised probe, without labels. A synthetic factor-model study with known parameters shows that conditioning improves the recovery of both market exposure and the asset-specific component.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.CE" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-10T04:40:04Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>23 pages, 4 figures, 14 tables</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <author>
      <name>Yuqi Wang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Fengyuan Liu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Mukai Li</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Haochen Luo</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Lei Li</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Qi Liu</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11166v2</id>
    <title>DexForesight: Grounding Dexterous Policies with Implicit Spatial Foresight</title>
    <updated>2026-11-10T07:28:24Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11166v2" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.11166v2" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Dexterous manipulation requires rich spatial foresight over both the surrounding scene and the robot hand. Existing world-action models often acquire such predictive knowledge through explicit future prediction. We introduce DexForesight, a framework that learns spatial foresight implicitly by injecting dynamics-aware world representations into the latent space of a dexterous policy. During training, DexForesight learns an action-conditioned spatial world model from offline scene depth, kinematic hand geometry, and demonstration trajectories. The world model predicts 3D hand-point motion and visual feature changes, capturing both articulated hand dynamics and scene changes under demonstrated actions, including the motion of fingers occluded from the cameras. We then freeze the world model and align policy queries with its predictive world queries, transferring the learned spatial dynamics into policy latents and using them to condition action generation. At deployment, only the policy is retained, operating on RGB observations, language, and robot state without depth input or explicit future prediction. On 11 DexJoCo simulation tasks, DexForesight improves the overall success rate of the base policy from 49.4% to 60.5%. On three real-robot dexterous manipulation tasks, DexForesight achieves 58.3% success, compared with 50.0% for the strongest baseline. Project page https://dexforesight.github.io/</summary>
    <category term="cs.RO" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-08T06:14:36Z</published>
