  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11740v1</id>
    <title>Creative Priming: Decoupling Process from Product to Escape the Creative Paradox</title>
    <updated>2026-11-09T01:28:05Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11740v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.11740v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Many of the most consequential advances promised by AI, from curing diseases and inventing new technologies to formulating new scientific theories, are fundamentally creative acts. Yet creativity is uniquely difficult to learn because it faces a creative paradox: learning to repeat past examples of creative success makes those specific examples more likely to recur, inhibiting the very creativity desired. This work argues that creativity is better modeled as an explicit process rather than as a property of individual artifacts, and proposes to learn the underlying generative principles behind the creative process. In particular, to escape the paradox, we introduce creative priming (CP), wherein a trained priming model generates a prompt-specific primer that assists a response model in being creative, explicitly decoupling the creative process from response generation. The priming model learns through GRPO, rewarding primers that lead to responses that are novel, high-quality, and diverse, thereby reinforcing the process that yields creative responses without reinforcing any specific response.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-09T01:28:05Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Alon Albalak</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Sebastian Gabriel</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>David B. D'Ambrosio</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Joel Lehman</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Kenneth O. Stanley</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11912v1</id>
    <title>LO-World: Unified BEV Latent Modeling for Joint 4D Occupancy and LiDAR Forecasting</title>
    <updated>2026-11-09T05:33:18Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11912v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.11912v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>LiDAR point clouds and 3D semantic occupancy represent two distinct modalities capturing fine-grained metric geometry and dense volumetric semantics, respectively, holding natural potential for mutual complementarity. However, existing world models typically forecast these modalities independently or cascade them heuristically, missing the opportunity for reciprocal cross-modal regularization. To address this, we propose \textbf{LO-World}, a unified 4D world model that forecasts both modalities within a shared BEV latent space. This single-stream formulation enables dense semantics and metric geometry to mutually reinforce each other, while eliminating multi-pipeline redundancy. Specifically, LO-World couples an efficient hybrid range-voxel autoencoder with a flow-matching generator that caches historical context to reduce iterative sampling latency. Extensive experiments demonstrate that LO-World outperforms prior state-of-the-art baselines, improving occupancy forecasting IoU by 10.9% and reducing LiDAR Chamfer Distance by 30.1%. Meanwhile, it sustains a real-time throughput exceeding $25$~FPS on a single RTX 4090. Our code is available at https://github.com/lzzzzzm/LO-World.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-09T05:33:18Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>Zhimin Liao</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Ping Wei</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Kai Mao</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Shuaijia Chen</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05791v1</id>
    <title>FlyHash for LLM Agent Memory: An Empirical Validation and a New Application</title>
    <updated>2026-11-04T00:57:24Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05791v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.05791v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Large language model (LLM) agents increasingly rely on external memory stores to retain information across conversations, typically via dense vector embeddings searched with approximate nearest-neighbor methods. The fruit fly's olfactory circuit computes a locality-sensitive hash of sensory input using a strikingly different mechanism - sparse random projection followed by winner-take-all competition - and prior work (Dasgupta et al., 2017) showed this scheme, dubbed FlyHash, preserves neighbor structure better per bit than conventional random-hyperplane hashing (SimHash) on several benchmarks. We apply FlyHash, to our knowledge for the first time, to LLM agent memory retrieval, and ask three questions a practitioner actually needs answered: does the quality advantage hold under a correctly matched-bit protocol (it does, robustly, across 10 seeds and four datasets spanning two modalities, with one specific, previously undocumented comparison pitfall identified and corrected along the way); does that advantage translate into real memory and latency savings, including against the dense-vector indexing (HNSW) practitioners default to today (no: at equal bits FlyHash's codes are larger and slower to compare than SimHash's, and HNSW dominates both hash methods at ordinary scale; a sparse-set comparison we introduce recovers most but not all of a naive latency gap, leaving hashing's practical case narrower and more specific than the quality result alone implies); and does the advantage survive genuine, non-circular agent-memory relevance labels rather than the nearest-neighbor-by-embedding proxy this literature otherwise relies on (it does). We report all three answers precisely, including the two that narrow rather than flatter the headline result, and release our implementation, a working agent-memory demo, and a full account of the methodological pitfalls we found and corrected.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-04T00:57:24Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>19 pages. Code, data and interactive demo: https://github.com/mohanji/flyhash-agent-memory (archived at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23903302)</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Mohan Bheemadolu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Chrisogonas O. Odhiambo</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12885v1</id>
    <title>World In-Context Learning for Agentic Robot Manipulation</title>
    <updated>2026-11-09T18:59:45Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12885v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.12885v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Trained action policies write manipulation skill into network weights, tying what is learned to the distribution it is learned from. In-context learning (ICL) avoids this coupling but has not matched trained policies, because a demonstration recorded in another scene carries no feedback from the world the robot currently faces. We observe that what an agent learns in context is semantic, recording task steps and object roles rather than visual appearance, so experience gathered in one world transfers to another. Based on this insight, we propose World In-Context Learning (WICL), a paradigm in which a frozen model starts from a demonstration, rehearses the task in a world such as a simulator, and writes the feedback into its context rather than its weights. We build RoboScript, a modular infrastructure that implements WICL for agentic manipulation. From a single demonstration, RoboScript learns each task through rehearsal in simulation, freezes the learned knowledge as a Task Memory, and deploys the frozen memory on new scenes with data isolation that ensures every deployment input is available on a real robot. On all 50 tasks of RoboTwin 2.0, RoboScript reaches 83.4% mean success across clean and randomized settings with one demonstration per task and no gradient updates, exceeding the strongest trained action policy by over 20 points. The method generalizes across visual distributions, improves through reflection, scales with the foundation model, and transfers to a physical dual-arm robot through cross-embodiment memory adaptation. With a world in the loop, a robot can learn a manipulation task in its context as well as it learns one in its weights. All code is available at https://github.com/ArcheOne-AI/RoboScript</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.RO" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-09T18:59:45Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Project Page: https://ArcheOne-AI.github.io/RoboScript</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>Heng Fang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Zehui Guo</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yuehan Zhang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Mingyang Du</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Dingkang Liang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Xiang Bai</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12762v1</id>
    <title>Sage-Taiyi: A Metacognitive Architecture for Scientific Discovery</title>
    <updated>2026-11-09T17:38:05Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12762v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.12762v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>We introduce Sage-Taiyi, a metacognition-inspired architecture that uses accumulated scientific understanding to guide what to investigate next and how to investigate it. Through explicit monitoring and control, the system revises its understanding of evidence, assumptions, and unresolved questions, then uses these assessments to adapt research targets and select reusable reasoning methods. On Taiyi-Bench, an AI co-scientist ideation benchmark comprising 265 tasks from 50 papers across ten disciplines, Sage-Taiyi ranks first on all eight metrics of historical rediscovery and open-ended ideation, achieving an Overall score of 54.0 compared with 47.9 for the strongest baseline, AI Scientist-v2. It also performs well on two external scientific ideation benchmarks. Five expert-guided collaborations further connect its proposals to experimental and computational validation across chemistry, materials science, and engineering. In chemistry, reaction-condition optimization raises the yield of cobalt-catalyzed carbonylation from trace amounts to 84% and photocatalytic allylic amination from 13% to 69%. These results support metacognitive regulation as an approach to scientific ideation and its progression toward practical validation.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-09T17:38:05Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>38 pages, 18 figures. Project homepage: https://alpha-innovator.github.io/Sage-Taiyi/</arxiv:comment>
