  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10592v1</id>
    <title>MotionGraphicsBench: Evaluating Motion Graphics Generation with Programmatic Verification</title>
    <updated>2026-11-07T08:59:49Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10592v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.10592v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Large language models can generate motion graphics from static images and natural language requests, but it remains difficult to evaluate how well models can follow requests that combine spatial, appearance, and temporal requirements. We introduce MotionGraphicsBench, an expert-curated benchmark of 100 instances for animating an input static SVG with a motion edit prompt. By supplying all required visual elements, our editing task reflects a designer-like workflow and isolates motion generation from graphic generation. Derived from real-world Lottie motion designs, our benchmark contains diverse combinations of spatio-temporal animation behaviors. Each benchmark instance pairs its model input with a deterministic, human-readable verification program that accepts different valid implementations and reports which prompt requirements pass or fail, rather than comparing against a reference animation or using a VLM judge. We construct these programs with a multi-agent pipeline using human-labeled animations and synthesized test cases. On a balanced test set of 180 animations, our programs achieve 93.3% raw agreement with human judgments, compared with 83.3% for a GPT-6 Astra VLM baseline, and outperform the baseline in both approval and rejection recall. Across 47 configurations from 32 LLMs and 12 providers, pass@1 ranges from 6.0% to 67.7%. Across 15 models evaluated at their highest available reasoning effort with 32 attempts per instance, additional attempts improve every model, but pass@32 still ranges from 38.0% to 95.0%. Our analysis of verification programs reveals that generated animations often fail to match reference poses, follow requested motion trajectories, or coordinate related events. We release our benchmark at https://huggingface.co/datasets/figma-ai/motion-graphics-bench.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-07T08:59:49Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>Jiaju Ma</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Ashvini Kumar Jindal</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Chuhua Wang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Sumithra Bhakthavatsalam</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14256v1</id>
    <title>A Cross-Scenario Survey and Analysis of Self-Evolving Multimodal Agents</title>
    <updated>2026-11-10T16:04:02Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14256v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.14256v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Recent work shows that multimodal agents increasingly self-improve without recurring human annotation. However, each self-improvement mechanism is designed and evaluated within a single scenario, leaving its preconditions unverified under cross-scenario transfer. This survey investigates the structural source of asymmetric mechanism transfer across four scenarios: multimodal reasoning, multimodal generation, GUI and Web navigation, and embodied manipulation. We first formalize the self-evolving multimodal agent by four criteria and a differentia of self-reference, and adopt the three-stage self-evolving loop of experience generation, feedback acquisition, and update consolidation as our analytical framework to position the scenarios along ceilings on rollout count and feedback fidelity. Comparing self-evolving with fixed loops within each scenario separates the limitations that the scenario imposes from those inherent in the self-evolving design. We then recast each mechanism as a trade-off that raises a scarce factor by presupposing a task-structural resource, such as output equivalence or checkable outcomes, and judge it portable, conditional, blocked, or redundant across scenarios. We attribute the asymmetry to divergent combinations of scarce factors and resources across opposite transfer directions, and identify the matched-budget measurements that could overturn these still untested verdicts.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-10T16:04:02Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>44 pages, 15 tables, 4 figures</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Jingjing Jiang</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14116v1</id>
    <title>Visual Similarity in Adult Word Extension and Artificial Word Learning</title>
    <updated>2026-11-10T14:07:17Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14116v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.14116v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>When two objects share a name, how much does their visual similarity explain, and what do the teaching examples add? First, we reanalyzed trial-level data from a large adult word-extension study (Lewis &amp; Frank, 2018), computing each test image's distance to the examples shown in the space of a frozen self-supervised visual encoder (DINOv2). Distance was associated with extension (log odds -3.23 per standard deviation; 520 people), and an exploratory post hoc analysis suggested that this association persisted within the taught domain after controlling for taxonomic level and teaching condition. Example composition also mattered: after three examples, adults extended a word to 1.1% of superordinate items when the examples were subordinate and to 77.5% when they were superordinate. Second, in an artificial learner using the same encoder, words joining two categories were learned less accurately the farther apart the categories were (84.7%, 65.4%, and 53.0% naming accuracy for near, mid, and far pairs). The encoder's distances correlated with human similarity and confusability measures from three datasets (rho = .501-.795), but this representational alignment did not translate into conclusive evidence that human-derived distances predict learner difficulty, and a second encoder trained on one child's experience reproduced the tier ordering but not the pair-level effect. The secondary human data were analyzed under an internal plan fixed before the confirmatory analyses (not externally preregistered). Visual similarity accounts for part of how generalization is organized in both systems, while the composition of examples contributes what the distance we used does not capture.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-10T14:07:17Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>15 pages, 5 figures. Code: https://github.com/patriciomvera/lexical-consensus . Data: https://huggingface.co/datasets/MrPatoVera/lexical-consensus-data and https://huggingface.co/datasets/MrPatoVera/lexical-consensus-data-cifar10h</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <author>
      <name>Patricio M. Vera</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14083v1</id>
    <title>Sable: A Small-Field Sumcheck Argument for Bit-Exact Inference of Large Language Models</title>
    <updated>2026-11-10T13:47:29Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14083v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.14083v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>We present Sable, an argument system and single-GPU prover for the statement that a committed, integer-quantized transformer produced a given token sequence from a given prompt, bit for bit. Sable fixes an integer function F_O with no floating-point step, in which every rounding, normalization, softmax and sampling decision is determined by its inputs, and proves it with a constraint system whose relations provably never wrap modulo the 31-bit KoalaBear prime. The proof batches zerochecks and LogUp-GKR lookups into one sumcheck per domain, and commits to every witness tensor and weight in one multilinear commitment built from Reed-Solomon codes, BLAKE3 Merkle trees and a mixed-length BaseFold opening. The prover streams the model layer by layer on one GPU. On one NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell GPU, Sable proves all 64 layers of Qwen2.5-32B in 92.5 s for 4,096 tokens and 398 s for 16,384 tokens, and covers Qwen3-32B and the mixture-of-experts Qwen3-30B-A3B (4,096 tokens in 121.8 and 84.7 s). A proof costs 41 to 50 times the serving time, or 6.4 cents per 4,096-token request at public GPU prices, and 40 to 64 times less time per parameter and token than zkLLM. On WikiText-2, F_O's greedy token matches the BF16 model's at 86.3% of positions; on Qwen3, F_O is within 1.6 points of BF16 on MMLU and 1.7 points on GSM8K. A verifier with a compiled core checks a proof on one CPU core in 19 to 56 s. In the random-oracle model, the interactive proof has round-by-round soundness error 2^-103. The proof is not zero-knowledge, so the system targets open-weight models. Code: https://github.com/moonmath-ai/Sable</summary>
    <category term="cs.CR" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-10T13:47:29Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CR"/>
    <author>
      <name>Tomer Solberg</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13907v1</id>
    <title>EVAThink: Evidence Sufficiency-Guided Training for Adaptive Thinking in Video Understanding</title>
    <updated>2026-11-10T11:01:57Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13907v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.13907v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Under a fixed frame budget, video understanding with vision-language models (VLMs) depends on whether the sampled frames provide sufficient evidence for the question. Sufficient frame evidence supports direct answering, whereas less informative observations may benefit from reasoning that connects the available cues. We introduce EVAThink, which enhances video understanding through evidence sufficiency-guided training for adaptive thinking. First, Recoverable Evidence Gap Mining constructs evidence-dense and evidence-sparse video views that share the same question and answer under an identical frame budget. It retains pairs where sparse views yield lower direct-answer accuracy than dense views but benefit from explicit reasoning, providing supervision for response-mode selection. Second, Evidence-Guided Two-Stage Reinforcement Learning uses stage-specific rewards to guide training with evidence-based mode preferences and then refine response efficiency, while first-token advantage regularization mitigates mode collapse. Trained on only 6.7K curated samples, EVAThink consistently improves upon the base model across video understanding benchmarks under both 32- and 64-frame budgets, while outperforming strong baselines on average. The code, model, and datasets are available at \href{https://github.com/ChangtiWu/EVAThink}{EVAThink}.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-10T11:01:57Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>The code, model, and datasets are available at \href{https://github.com/ChangtiWu/EVAThink}{EVAThink}</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>Changti Wu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Ruizhe Fan</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Bin Wang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Lei Zhang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yuxiang Zhang</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14320v1</id>
    <title>Don't Just Trust Your Memory: Evidence-Linked Memory Reduces Memory-Induced Degradation in LLM Agents</title>
    <updated>2026-11-10T16:46:59Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14320v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.14320v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>External memory enables LLM agents to improve from experience by distilling trajectories of past tasks into concise lessons for new tasks. However, memory can also lead to memory-induced degradation, where an agent fails on tasks it would solve without memory, often because it follows a lesson that is incorrect, or misapplies or misreads a valid one. This is because lessons are inferred from trajectories labeled only with task success or failure, yet are typically provided without the trajectories they came from, leaving the agent no way to check them. To address this, we introduce WikiTrace, an evidence-linked memory system designed to let agents check each lesson against its original trajectory. Specifically, motivated by the finding that a trajectory often still holds the evidence needed to catch an erroneous lesson, WikiTrace stores each lesson as a concise insight linked to a summary of why its trajectory succeeded or failed and to the raw trajectory itself. Then, on a new task, the agent starts from all insights and, whenever one lacks details needed to act, conflicts with another, or comes from a failed task, descends through its links with terminal tools to fill in missing details or correct the insight before acting. We validate WikiTrace on five agentic benchmarks, showing that it consistently outperforms existing memory approaches and a baseline given the same insights alone, while also reducing memory-induced degradation. Our code is available at https://github.com/yukyeongleee/wikitrace</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.MA" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-10T16:46:59Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Submitted to ACL Rolling Review (October 2026 cycle); under review</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Yukyeong Lee</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yumin Choi</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Jinheon Baek</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Sung Ju Hwang</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13348v1</id>
    <title>ProTEUS: A mutation-resolved world model for closed-loop protein evolution</title>
    <updated>2026-11-09T23:19:33Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13348v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.13348v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Protein engineering requires navigating rugged fitness landscapes, where small sequence changes can produce large functional differences, but experimental screening can only sample a small fraction of sequence space. Thus, a central challenge is to efficiently traverse the large combinatorial space of mutations in silico. We introduce ProTEUS, a mutation-resolved world model for closed-loop protein evolution. Pretrained on deep mutational scanning assays, ProTEUS learns a joint representation of sequence, evolutionary, and structural information using MutationFormer, a mutation-biased attention architecture. Variants are represented as states in this joint latent space, and mutations define actions whose functional consequences are predicted. To support exploration of many mutants, we developed a fast, physics-constrained all-atom predictor that utilizes explicit geometric constraint enforcement to achieve &gt;10 fold increase in speed than AlphaFold3 with guaranteed physical validity. Lastly, ProTEUS refines its prediction and understanding of protein activity through closed loop experimental feedback to guide evolution toward a desired phenotype. ProTEUS surpasses established models in both zero-shot and supervised fine-tuning mutation prediction tasks. We experimentally demonstrate its utility in two closed-loop multi-objective engineering campaigns on aldehyde dehydrogenase and levansucrase, yielding variants with &gt;10 fold higher activity than wild type. ProTEUS provides a world-model framework that connects virtual evaluation of mutational outcomes with experimental learning for efficient protein evolution.</summary>
    <category term="q-bio.BM" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-09T23:19:33Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>64 pages, 5 main figures, 5 supplementary figures</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="q-bio.BM"/>
    <author>
      <name>Yikun Zhang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Minghao Guo</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Guangxue Xu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Peter Yichen Chen</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Wojciech Matusik</name>
    </author>
