  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01527v1</id>
    <title>From Tool State to Scientific Action: Auditing Decision Provenance in Biomedical Research Agents</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T03:58:26Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01527v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.01527v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>A scientific agent can give the same verdict while reading and citing different evidence. BioAgent-DF tests this separation using paired reference and candidate values saved at the same scientific computation site. It restores or transplants those values at their original coordinates, then runs the agent on healthy hardware. Across eight SciFact batches, Qwen3.5-4B shows repeatable citation changes in three capture-claim combinations, and Gemma 3 4B IT shows repeatable read and citation changes in four. These seven combinations cover two claims. Full restoration recovers the reference evidence sets while verdicts remain unchanged. Verdict changes observed under the baseline prompt did not recur under the explicit-example prompt. Complementary tool and optimizer experiments distinguish recovery of numerical state, recovery of ranked lists, and restoration of an agent's visible history. These results show that an audit should track both the evidence an agent uses and its final answer. In hERG cardiac-safety assay allocation, silent numerical faults changed testing decisions while the fixed-budget shortlist kept the same number of compounds labeled as blockers. After the agent had used faulty values, restoring its earlier execution state and supplying certified scores recovered all 39 changed decisions, whereas replacing the prediction file recovered none; no incorrect action was committed. A streaming implementation of the arithmetic checks reduced the total time for forward execution and verification by 42.4% relative to the original FP64 implementation, with identical flags and recovery outputs.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="q-bio.QM" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-01T03:58:26Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Accepted at NeurIPS 2026 AgenticLS Workshop; adds one experiment</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Xingyu Yi</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Xinya Zhang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yunshen Zhou</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Haoyu Bao</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10866v1</id>
    <title>The Surprising Effectiveness of Deleting Weights in LLM Post-Training</title>
    <updated>2026-11-07T17:33:20Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10866v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.10866v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>How little of a pretrained large language model (LLM) has to change for it to acquire new capabilities? We find that zeroing fewer than 0.05% of its weights, with no other modification, matches full fine-tuning (FFT) at scale across three LLM post-training regimes: reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards, supervised fine-tuning, and on-policy distillation. Our method, Deletion Tuning, learns a binary mask over the gate projection of transformer blocks. Two practical advantages follow: the trained mask is hundreds to thousands of times smaller than the corresponding FFT checkpoint, and Deletion Tuning forgets far less, which addresses a central challenge in continual learning. Under reinforcement learning, its prior-task accuracy stays near the base level while FFT, LoRA, and random sparse fine-tuning lose up to 5.4 points. To understand why masking alone suffices, we analyze the geometry of the learned weight changes under reinforcement learning using stable rank, spectrum drift, and principal-weight overlap. Compared with LoRA and random sparse fine-tuning, Deletion Tuning is closest to FFT across the three measures.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-07T17:33:20Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>NeurIPS 2026</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <author>
      <name>Jack Lu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Zhenbang Yang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Mike Lasby</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Tejas Pote</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yani Ioannou</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Mengye Ren</name>
