  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12527v1</id>
    <title>WaveGen: Truly End-to-End Waveform Generation via Internal Spectral Trajectory Learning</title>
    <updated>2026-11-09T15:02:29Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12527v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.12527v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>This work proposes WaveGen, a fully end-to-end diffusion-based text-to-waveform framework based on Spectral Forcing, which jointly learns an internal spectral trajectory and the target waveform trajectory within a single model, enabling direct end-to-end text-to-waveform generation. Unlike recent TTS pipelines, WaveGen does not rely on separately trained or externally decoded intermediate representations, such as neural audio codecs, Mel-spectrogram vocoders, or VAE-based latent models. In addition, WaveGen performs internal text-speech alignment within a single model, eliminating the need for external alignment modules such as duration predictors. To preserve a truly end-to-end formulation, our framework further avoids dependence on self-supervised representations, such as BERT-based text embeddings and Wav2Vec 2.0-based semantic representations, as well as semantic distillation methods for training optimization. To achieve this, we carefully design Spectral Forcing, a model architecture with disentangled diffusion heads, and a training objective based on a multi-scale spectral $v$-loss. We further validate the scalability of end-to-end TTS models by scaling the model size from 0.1B to 0.6B parameters. Using a single architecture, open-source data, and one-stage training, without external modules such as duration predictor or REPA, WaveGen demonstrates fully end-to-end waveform generation achieving state-of-the-art performance on the Emilia training dataset. In particular, WaveGen achieves a WER of 1.47 and a SIM of 0.75 on the Seed-en benchmark. Source code and checkpoints are available at https://github.com/sh-lee-prml/WaveGen</summary>
    <category term="cs.SD" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="eess.AS" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="eess.SP" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-09T15:02:29Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Accepted to NeurIPS 2026</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.SD"/>
    <author>
      <name>Sang-Hoon Lee</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Ha-Yeong Choi</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12411v1</id>
    <title>PhysOPSD: Code as Privilege for Physically Plausible Video Generation</title>
    <updated>2026-11-09T13:43:37Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12411v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.12411v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>While modern video generative models achieve remarkable visual fidelity, they remain noticeably brittle when generating physically plausible dynamics and complex interactions. Targeted post-training is crucial, yet supervised fine-tuning suffers from offline state mismatch, and reinforcement learning is hindered by sparse, ill-defined physical reward functions. On-Policy (Self-)Distillation (OPSD) reconciles on-policy rollouts with dense corrective feedback, shifting the core challenge to designing effective privileged context. However, conventional modalities fall short: text prompts are inherently declarative, whereas real reference videos heavily entangle physical laws with visual appearance without parametric control. To this end, we propose PhysOPSD, introducing executable code as privileged physical context for video generation. Unlike static visual observations, executable code instantiates an editable physical world with intrinsic abstraction and controllability. Powered by PhysOPSD-Engine, we translate real-world physical scenarios into verifiable simulation programs. PhysOPSD then incorporates (i) dual-level physical privilege to bridge the information gap via an intermediate bottleneck, alongside (ii) controlled physical variations under shared rollout noise to expose causal dynamic shifts. Extensive experiments demonstrate that PhysOPSD substantially improves physical plausibility across diverse backbones, outperforming SFT and RL baselines with only $3$K samples, while preserving general video generation quality.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-09T13:43:37Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Project: https://haroldchen19.github.io/PhysOPSD-site/</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>Harold Haodong Chen</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Rui Peng</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Lingteng Qiu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Kejie Qiu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Chao Xu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Zilong Dong</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09195v1</id>
    <title>InstantHMR : Real-Time Human Mesh Recovery on Your Phone</title>
    <updated>2026-11-06T09:17:27Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09195v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.09195v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Real-time 3D human mesh recovery from a single image on mobile devices is a challenging problem with many applications, including robotics, biomechanics, AR/VR, and exergames. Current state-of-the-art models are either too slow to run efficiently on mobile devices or sacrifice accuracy for speed. We present InstantHMR, a family of open-source single-image regressors suited for mobile inference, based on the Momentum Human Rig (MHR) parametric model and trained exclusively on publicly available annotations from the SAM 3D Body dataset. Our approach achieves remarkable performance on a Samsung Galaxy S23 NPU, recovering a 6,890-vertex body mesh in 5.5 ms per person, and 0.98 ms on an NVIDIA RTX 4070 GPU. This speed surpasses all existing methods we evaluated. On mobile, it is 12x faster than the single mobile-compatible method that slightly outperforms ours in accuracy, while achieving competitive accuracy with 40.0 mm PA-MPJPE on 3DPW, 48.8 mm on EMDB and 41.7 mm on RICH. To ensure fair comparisons, we re-evaluate 11 state-of-the-art methods under a unified protocol aligned with the closest MHR-based competitors, including Meta's official MHR-to-SMPL conversion, and report their reproduction results. Additionally, we measure latency across eight different targets, ranging from desktop TensorRT to two phone NPUs. Code and models are available at https://github.com/Naturalpad-studio/InstantHMR.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-06T09:17:27Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>Mohamed Adjel</name>
      <arxiv:affiliation>LAAS-GEPETTO</arxiv:affiliation>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Kahina Chalabi</name>
      <arxiv:affiliation>LAAS-GEPETTO</arxiv:affiliation>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Vincent Bonnet</name>
      <arxiv:affiliation>LAAS-GEPETTO</arxiv:affiliation>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10329v1</id>
    <title>SIRIS: Can LLMs Tell When Simulation Errors Matter in AI-Driven Systems Research</title>
    <updated>2026-11-06T23:55:52Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10329v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.10329v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>LLM agents increasingly drive systems research and rely on simulators to evaluate candidate designs at scale. Simulators are imperfect by design: their modeling limitations can distort predictions yet leave the recommended design correct, or change which design should be selected. We introduce Siris to test whether LLMs can tell these cases apart, judging from their systems understanding when a limitation invalidates the simulator's recommendation. By perturbing a simulator, Siris constructs workload pairs with the same candidates, limitation, and simulator recommendation. The perturbation changes predictions on both workloads, yet a reference simulator supports the recommendation on only one. Evaluating thirteen frontier LLMs on LLM serving design problems, we find that identifying a limitation does not ensure a correct choice, and even the strongest models sometimes abandon valid recommendations. Self-correction through a review turn helps these models restore valid choices but introduces new errors in several others. These findings motivate evaluating systems understanding through design decisions under imperfect simulators.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-06T23:55:52Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>21 pages, including references and appendices</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Jinwoo Hwang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Sukmin Cho</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Tao Zhang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Soheil Abbasloo</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Jongse Park</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Peng Cheng</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10015v1</id>
    <title>Micius-Flash: Lightweight Agentic Intelligence for Engineering</title>
    <updated>2026-11-04T17:53:55Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10015v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.10015v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>We introduce Micius-Flash, a 27-billion-parameter model for engineering with native multimodal input, deep reasoning and a 256K-token context window. We design the Micius data pipeline as a knowledge-tree-driven data flywheel comprising three stages: knowledge tree construction and expansion, data synthesis, and quality validation with hard-example augmentation. From this data, we train expert models for semiconductor knowledge and reasoning, hardware coding, and semiconductor multimodal understanding, along with agentic experts. Subsequently, we employ Multi-Teacher On-Policy Distillation (MOPD) to supervise training on sequences generated by Micius itself, thereby integrating the capabilities of these expert models into a unified model. On more than 20 datasets with about 15K records, Micius-Flash averages 64.2% on hardware code benchmarks, 15.0 points above a same-size baseline. It scores 62.4% on a semiconductor-specific agent benchmark, exceeding the same-size baseline by 17.2 points. We further develop Micius Harness for industrial applications, connecting the model to specialized tools, including EDA tools and KiCad, as well as execution environments. Case studies in PCB design, chip design workflow automation, and software development demonstrate the model's ability to use tool execution feedback for multi-step planning, code debugging, and iterative verification. We release Micius as an open-source model to advance the development of agents for science and engineering.</summary>
    <category term="cs.SE" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
