  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12886v1</id>
    <title>Reframe: Observation-Aligned Multi-Teacher On-Policy Distillation for Agentic Visual Reasoning</title>
    <updated>2026-11-09T18:59:54Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12886v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.12886v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Agentic reinforcement learning (RL) and multi-teacher on-policy distillation (MOPD) offer complementary routes to post-training. For visual agents, however, tool use presents two related challenges. Models may overlook useful tool-returned observations, while poorly grounded actions can limit the evidence available for subsequent distillation. We introduce Reframe, a post-training framework that treats the observation state, rather than only the output distribution, as an object of alignment. First, Ground-Drop-GRPO (GD-GRPO) trains spatial and temporal specialists with evidence-aware rewards and stochastic removal of the original visual input, encouraging them to acquire grounded evidence and use the returned observations. Second, Observation-Gated-MOPD (OG-MOPD) replaces deficient tool actions with canonical ones mid-rollout and resumes distillation from the repaired observation. The two stages complement each other, as learning to rely on tool-returned observations can help the policy benefit from observation repair. Neither stage requires a task-specific SFT cold start. Extensive experiments over 14 image and video benchmarks demonstrate that Reframe outperforms both Mix-RL and vanilla MOPD. Ablation results show that GD-GRPO increases observation dependence and tool gain compared with outcome-based GRPO.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-09T18:59:54Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>Haozhi Yuan</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Handong Li</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yifan Zhang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Qianshan Wei</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12865v1</id>
    <title>Shortcuts and Boundaries: Verifiable RL Misalignment and Contextual Safety Learning</title>
    <updated>2026-11-09T18:52:20Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12865v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.12865v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Reinforcement learning (RL) can improve an agent's task capability while reinforcing behavior that violates contextual execution requirements. We study this reward-driven misalignment, including reward hacking, through verifiable reward--context conflicts, where compliant task completion remains feasible while some executable shortcuts earn more reward than every compliant strategy. Encoding these conflicts in task dynamics lets us generate diverse interactive environments at scale and derive compliance feedback from execution histories. In resource and reporting workflows, task optimization increases real productivity through unauthorized resource use or raises reported scores while actual work declines. Controlled changes in the training distribution show that minority conflict exposure can still lead to widespread shortcut acquisition, reaching 97.9\% certified profitable violations on an API task under a 25\% conflict mixture after 128 updates. The same contextual checks supply a simple binary penalty that supports joint capability and compliance gains and partially mitigates acquired violations. Learned safety adapts to expanded authorization and transfers across mechanisms into workflows with evolving evidence and approval requirements. On 32 unseen changing-evidence tasks, resource-trained safety increases safe completion from 53.1\% to 89.1\% relative to task-only training, while both policies achieve full real work. These results connect a verifiable account of reward-driven misalignment with scalable task construction and safety learning that remains useful under changing execution requirements.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.CR" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-09T18:52:20Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Project Web: https://xinjie-shen.com/post/rlmisalignment</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Xinjie Shen</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10649v1</id>
    <title>MACaQ: Fast, Model Agnostic Calibrated Quantization for Low-Precision Weights and Activations</title>
    <updated>2026-11-07T10:49:02Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10649v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.10649v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Weight-activation quantization can reduce the memory and compute cost of neural-network inference, but recent methods often require expensive optimization and architecture-specific propagation of basis transforms across layers. We introduce MACaQ, an MXFP4 W4A4 post-training quantization method that treats every linear layer independently. MACaQ applies a per-channel scale and a block-Hadamard transform online to each layer's activations, while absorbing the corresponding inverse transforms into its weights. Retaining these transforms online avoids cross-layer dependencies and enables a simple streaming implementation of the quantization process, in which only one layer must reside in memory at a time. To improve over round-to-nearest quantization, we introduce Flip-Refinement, a Hessian-aware coordinate-descent procedure that greedily moves individual FP4 codes to adjacent representable values. Despite executing the activation transforms at inference time, MACaQ adds only 2.3% end-to-end overhead over pure MXFP4 on Qwen3-32B in vLLM. MACaQ reaches lower perplexity than DuQuant++ and MR-GPTQ, and already matches their best perplexity on Llama-3-8B in 11 times less quantization time. We further apply MACaQ without architecture-specific modifications to an image diffusion model, illustrating the generality of its layer-local design and show quantization of a 122B parameter model in 64 minutes, using only 32.5GB GPU RAM.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-07T10:49:02Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <author>
      <name>Lorenz K. Muller</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Philippe Bich</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Chiara Boretti</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Hyun-Min Chang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Jiawei Zhuang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Lukas Cavigelli</name>
    </author>
