  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14488v1</id>
    <title>Is Your Robot's Memory Just a Clock? Temporal Shortcuts in Memory-Augmented VLA Policies</title>
    <updated>2026-11-10T18:47:57Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14488v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.14488v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Memory-augmented vision-language-action (VLA) policies are meant to remember what happened. Many of them, however, fill memory by time: they sample frames uniformly across the episode and stamp each frame with its time step. Because time passes even when nothing happens, such a memory may not tell waiting from working. We show that it does not. On the counting tasks of RoboMME, we make the robot briefly pause or fidget its wrist. Such a null event changes nothing the task depends on, yet a released memory VLA counts the interval as work done and stops early. We call this failure phantom progress. It cuts success from 47 to 16 of 50 on one task and from 44 to 10 on the other. When the same frames are withheld from memory, success returns, so the cause lies in memory rather than in the interruption itself. We trace the failure to a temporal shortcut: because demonstrations proceed at a steady pace, elapsed time predicts progress. Indeed, rescaling the time stamps alone makes the policy stop early when the clock runs fast and repeat when it runs slow. Surprisingly, removing the time encoding does not cure the failure, because the frames of a null event still occupy slots of the time-sampled memory. Across RoboMME's released designs, vulnerability follows how memory is indexed. Of the fixes the diagnosis suggests, training with injected pauses does not remove the failure and impairs counting, whereas indexing memory by events recovers half or more of the loss without retraining. A memory meant to remember what happened should count events rather than clock ticks.</summary>
    <category term="cs.RO" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-10T18:47:57Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>38 pages</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.RO"/>
    <author>
      <name>Chubin Zhang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Bo An</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14115v1</id>
    <title>LEASH: Stay Safe in Agentic Robotics</title>
    <updated>2026-11-10T14:06:20Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14115v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.14115v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Foundation models such as GPT and Claude have shown strong capabilities in spatial understanding and embodied intelligence. These advances make agentic robotics a promising path toward artificial general intelligence in robotics. However, we find that using foundation models to control robots poses serious safety risks, and comprehensive safety evaluation is still lacking. We introduce RoboRisk, a comprehensive benchmark for evaluating safety in agentic robotics. Its 92 simulated tasks and 13 physical-robot tasks fall into five categories: Task-level Refusal (T), Plan Repair (P), Execution-time Hazard (E), Injection (I), and Sequential Memory (S). Prior benchmarks cover at most two of these categories, and several evaluate only text outputs. In contrast, RoboRisk covers all five and executes every task in closed loop. We evaluate six state-of-the-art foundation models on RoboRisk: GPT-6 Astra, GPT-6.1 Sol, GPT-6 Luna, Claude Opus 5.5, Gemini 4 Argon, and Grok 4.7. These models generally show high harm rates. We also evaluate two robot-specific APIs: the Dream API and PokeBot's RoboAPI. We further propose the Lease-Enforced Agentic Safety Harness (LEASH), which offers essential safety infrastructure for agentic robotics and can be integrated into nearly any agentic architecture. LEASH leverages a decision model (Jev or OpenAI's Decisions API) to implement task, action, and commit gates, which review the task, each action, and each irreversible operation in their visual context. The planner and all three gates share a common safety policy. Experiments on RoboRisk in simulation and on physical robots show that LEASH effectively reduces harm across all six foundation models. It largely preserves their task completion performance and adds little time and token overhead.</summary>
    <category term="cs.RO" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-10T14:06:20Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.RO"/>
    <author>
      <name>Qinfeng Zhu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Weiguang Zhao</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Ke Sun</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Xihao Guo</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yunxi Jiang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Rui Zhang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Lei Fan</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Kaizhu Huang</name>
