  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.42148v1</id>
    <title>Flide: Accelerating Decision Inference with Exact State Forking</title>
    <updated>2026-10-30T17:59:38Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.42148v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.42148v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Decision models such as Jev have emerged as a highly promising paradigm for LLM agent systems, providing lightweight and reliable categorical reasoning for rapid System One execution. However, existing generation-oriented inference engines fail to support these workloads effectively, suffering from severe prefix recomputation overheads or distorted output probabilities. To address this challenge, we propose Flide, an efficient and exact inference engine that decouples the logical tree structure of a decision request from its physical execution within a single flattened token stream. Through ForkAttention, Flide navigates request dependencies using GPU-resident indices across both standard attention and recurrent Gated DeltaNet layers, guaranteeing in exact arithmetic that every query branch evaluates identically to an isolated forward pass. By expressing branch topologies directly in GPU-resident metadata, Flide accommodates diverse request mixtures with a compact set of pre-recorded execution graphs while enabling expanding agent histories to resume from checkpoints saved in earlier passes. Extensive evaluations on public hybrid and decision models demonstrate that Flide answers queries up to 9.5 times faster than vLLM and SGLang while staying closest to a float32 reference in total variation distance. By unifying physical execution throughput with mathematical exactness, Flide establishes a principled foundation for scalable, latency-critical decision intelligence in agent systems.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-30T17:59:38Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>20 pages. Project page: https://flide.org</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <author>
      <name>Bobo Li</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Jiajia Song</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Hao Fei</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Wynne Hsu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Mong Li Lee</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39671v1</id>
    <title>Incentivizing Self-Learning in Large Language Models via Reinforcement Learning</title>
    <updated>2026-10-29T09:21:26Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39671v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39671v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Large language models (LLMs) draw on parametric knowledge and retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) to solve knowledge-intensive tasks. Yet problem solving in unfamiliar domains calls for tightly coupled knowledge acquisition and reasoning, beyond factual retrieval and aggregation or learning solely from a preassembled context. We study self-learning -- autonomously retrieving, studying, and applying knowledge from long-form reference materials on the fly. To investigate this capability, we formulate a textbook-based mathematical problem-solving task. LLMs must navigate textbook exposition, learn and apply relevant principles, and construct proofs for exercises, with reference solutions withheld. We introduce a BookNavigator interface to support this human-inspired study process and incentivize self-learning through reinforcement learning (RL) with fine-grained rewards. Experiments show that our method improves performance across in-domain and out-of-domain textbooks and subjects, raising Intern-S2-Preview-35B's overall proof accuracy from 51.5% to 79.5% and outperforming RL-trained textbook-free and fixed-retrieval baselines. These gains extend to complex long-context benchmarks involving different task types, with improvements over the base model of 1.7 and 2.3 percentage points on BrowseComp-Plus and CL-bench, respectively. Together, these findings suggest that RL can foster a domain-agnostic self-learning strategy, highlighting the potential of self-learning from long-form corpora to strengthen broader problem-solving capabilities on complex, long-context tasks.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-29T09:21:26Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <author>
      <name>Lingkai Kong</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yuzhe Gu</name>
    </author>
