  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09987v1</id>
    <title>Stochastic Deep Supervision for Gallery-Consistent Early-Exit Face Recognition</title>
    <updated>2026-11-02T13:39:05Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09987v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.09987v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Vision Transformers (ViTs) perform strongly in face recognition (FR), yet their fixed-depth inference limits deployment in resource-constrained settings. We propose SD-ViT, a training framework that gives a usable exit at every block depth by making every depth produce discriminative embeddings, for budget-selected fixed-depth exits. Standard ViTs supervise intermediate layers only through backpropagation, so their intermediate representations are not directly optimized to form usable identity embeddings. SD-ViT adds a stochastic auxiliary signal: at each iteration a randomly selected intermediate block is passed through the same embedding head as the final layer and trained with a weighted margin-penalty softmax loss. The objective is thus stochastically sampled deep supervision through a shared classifier. Because both loss terms share that head and classifier, intermediate tokens are trained to map into the same embedding space as final-layer tokens. This matters for FR, where matching is by similarity: a probe processed at an early exit can be compared directly against full-depth gallery embeddings, with no gallery recomputation or adaptation layers. On established benchmarks, SD-ViT stays within 0.4 IJB-C points of the same backbone trained conventionally at full depth, while its early-exit probes recover over 96% of their own full-depth performance at 50% of the computational cost without modifying the reference gallery. A single verification threshold calibrated at full depth remains usable from quarter depth onward.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-02T13:39:05Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Accepted at WACV2027</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>Guray Ozgur</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Tahar Chettaoui</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Eduarda Caldeira</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Naser Damer</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Fadi Boutros</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08452v1</id>
    <title>ReproBridge: Building Reproducibility into ML Research Artifacts</title>
    <updated>2026-11-05T18:45:36Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08452v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.08452v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Reproducing machine learning experiments requires reconstructing procedures from information scattered across papers, released code, and supporting artifacts. For coding agents, reproduction success can therefore depend on their ability to fill in missing details, rather than on the released artifacts alone. We ask whether preserving recovered procedures and decisions improves subsequent independent reproduction. We define a standardized artifact design comprising a refined codebase, environment setup, experiment launchers, and a reproduction guide, and introduce ReproBridge, an agent workflow for constructing these refined reproducible artifacts. ReproBridge-Bench provides 1,029 tasks from 387 experiments across 36 papers, anchored by five human-verified reference scores per task. Agents attempt tasks in fresh sessions with raw code or refined artifacts; a separate Auditor checks execution evidence. Across four evaluated models, refined artifacts raise mean reproduction success from 76.4% to 92.6% and reduce downstream API spending by 19.7% on average. The cross-model success-rate spread narrows from 21.6 to 3.9 percentage points, with all four models achieving mean success rates above 90%. Removing any component reduces success in the ablation study, with the largest losses from code and environment removal. These results suggest that preserving execution knowledge helps even models with lower raw-code success reproduce experiments more reliably and at lower API cost.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-05T18:45:36Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Yubo Wang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Rui Meng</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Jaehyun Nam</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Tomas Pfister</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Jinsung Yoon</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08969v1</id>
    <title>PhysioWave-T: Token-Efficient Wavelet Foundation Models for Biosignals</title>
    <updated>2026-11-06T04:30:15Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08969v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.08969v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>We present PhysioWave-T, a token-efficient wavelet foundation model family for EEG, ECG, and sEMG. Dynamic scale folding recombines J+1 wavelet subbands prior to tokenization, compressing sequence length from (J+1)CS to CS tokens for J levels, C channels, and S temporal patches. Route-specific front ends and learned channel-name embeddings allow one Transformer per modality to handle diverse montages and sampling rates, while unseen setups reuse the backbone via lightweight adapters. We pre-train a 30M-parameter EEG model on over 15M windows (19--128 channels), alongside ECG (30M) and sEMG (12M) encoders. Across four EEG routes, scale folding cuts Transformer latency by 4.0--7.2x and peak memory by 6.8--12.7x over scale expansion without accuracy loss. Our EEG encoder leads 8 of 9 metrics against BENDR, BIOT, LaBraM, and EEGPT, and surpasses LUNA-L on wearable seizure detection with fewer parameters. Reliability-aware latent fusion (RALF) further integrates summary tokens from independently pre-trained encoders via sample-adaptive weights. On SeizeIT2, RALF raises event sensitivity at 11 false alarms/h from 61.4% to 74.4% and window AUPRC from 0.321 to 0.399 over the best unimodal baseline, outperforming weighted-logit and concatenation fusion across all variants. PhysioWave-T also achieves state-of-the-art sEMG accuracies and top macro-F1 on PTB-XL Super and Chapman-Shaoxing. Code and models: https://github.com/ForeverBlue816/PhysioWave.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-06T04:30:15Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>15 pages, 11 figures, 5 tables. Code: https://github.com/ForeverBlue816/PhysioWave , Models &amp; Weights: https://huggingface.co/collections/ForeverBlue/physiowave-t</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Yanlong Chen</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Simone Benatti</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Luca Benini</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Cuntai Guan</name>
    </author>
    <author>
