  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14516v1</id>
    <title>TorchCurves: Differentiable Parametric Curves</title>
    <updated>2026-11-10T18:58:52Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14516v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.14516v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>We present TorchCurves, a PyTorch library that treats vector-valued parametric curves as learnable primitives. Its central operation maps batches of scalar inputs to points on curves represented in B-spline or Legendre polynomial bases, with differentiation through both inputs and coefficients. Curve coefficients can be optimized jointly with the surrounding model or produced by another network. The library provides trainable modules, reusable basis modules, and a functional interface, supporting applications ranging from continuous feature embeddings to Kolmogorov-Arnold networks and functional regression. Its implementation combines classical evaluation algorithms with vectorized PyTorch operations, emphasizing readability and maintainability without custom hardware kernels. Timing experiments compare curve evaluation against NumPy and SciPy and KAN training against EfficientKAN across a range of problem sizes. The results illustrate where this generality remains computationally practical and where overhead is substantial. Library is available on GitHub at https://github.com/alexshtf/torchcurves.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="stat.ML" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-10T18:58:52Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Software paper for a library I've been working on for more than a year</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <author>
      <name>Alex Shtoff</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13482v1</id>
    <title>ExRL: Sample-Efficient Online Adaptation of Long-Chunk Policies via Execution Length Control</title>
    <updated>2026-11-10T02:44:05Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13482v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.13482v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Predicting long action chunks, often spanning dozens of timesteps, has become a key ingredient in increasingly capable robot policies. In this paper, we find that adapting these long-chunk policies with existing reinforcement learning (RL) methods can require many online samples due to the high dimensionality of the action chunks being optimized, making such adaptation impractical in real-world scenarios where interaction data are costly to collect. Our key insight is to shift adaptation from optimizing actions to optimizing how long to execute each predicted action chunk. This keeps the adaptation space low-dimensional while its expressivity grows with the chunk size. We thus introduce Execution at the Right Length (ExRL), a simple approach which augments the base policy with a lightweight adapter that predicts the execution length of each chunk and is trained using off-policy RL algorithms. Across simulation benchmarks and real-world robotic tasks, ExRL enables sample-efficient adaptation of a diverse range of long-chunk policies with minimal task-specific hyperparameter tuning.</summary>
    <category term="cs.RO" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-10T02:44:05Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.RO"/>
    <author>
      <name>Xu Luo</name>
    </author>
    <author>
