  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12184v1</id>
    <title>Blind Variation, Selective Retention: Improving Creative Idea Generation in AI Scientists</title>
    <updated>2026-11-09T10:36:38Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12184v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.12184v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Scientific discovery requires ideas that are original, useful, and non-obvious. Yet AI scientists are typically given as much task information as possible during idea generation, which can anchor generation toward familiar approaches. Inspired by the cognitive framework of Blind Variation and Selective Retention (BVSR), we propose a discovery framework that separates these two roles of information: it generates proposals under deliberately limited task information to broaden exploration, then learns from proposals previously verified on the same task and their outcomes to retain promising candidates for costly empirical evaluation. We evaluate BVSR across five proposer models and 12 scientific tasks. Limiting task information substantially increases proposal originality and non-obviousness, and produces a larger share of highly creative and high-utility proposals than existing state-of-the-art prompting-based baselines. Learning from prior proposal outcomes on the task further improves candidate selection, discarding half of the candidates while retaining most top-performing proposals. When incorporated into existing AI-scientist workflows, BVSR achieves stronger discoveries across gene expression analysis, drug response prediction, anomaly detection, scaling law discovery, and brain activity prediction. These results suggest that effective AI scientists should use information differently across stages of discovery: constrain task-specific information during exploration while exploiting accumulated empirical experience on the task.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-09T10:36:38Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Project Website: https://zou-group.github.io/BVSR/</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <author>
      <name>Guowei Xu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Jiaru Zou</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Mete Erdogan</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Haotian Ye</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Pan Lu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>James Zou</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12324v1</id>
    <title>How Many Exposures Does a World Model Need? Learning Unseen Game Mechanics at Test Time</title>
    <updated>2026-11-09T12:45:40Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12324v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.12324v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>World models enable agents to plan and learn from imagined experience, but in an unfamiliar environment they must learn its mechanics from limited interaction. A mechanic is a rule for how an action or an interaction changes part of the environment. Each occurrence of a mechanic that the model observes is one exposure. We ask how many exposures a world model needs to predict the effects of a new environment's mechanics. We address this question on ARC-AGI-3, a benchmark of interactive games defined by their programs. We introduce MechEval, which labels 69 recurring mechanics across 20 games from these programs. It scores each prediction on the cells the mechanic changes at each exposure. As a test bed, we build WARC (World model for ARC-AGI-3), a 35M-parameter vision transformer that is pretrained on other games and updates all of its parameters after each transition. On held-out games, WARC learns to predict four in five mechanics with at least 80% accuracy on the cells they change and keeps that accuracy to the end of the recordings. Half of them reach that accuracy within about 80 exposures, in a single play of the game, and without updates only one in seven does at all. In closed-loop play on the public games, WARC-Agent, which pairs WARC with a fixed graph explorer, triples the explorer's official efficiency score from 0.15 to 0.46, mostly through test-time training. On the hidden games of the Kaggle leaderboard, our entry scores 0.20.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-09T12:45:40Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>31 pages, 21 figures, 12 tables. ARC Prize 2026 Paper Track. Code and weights: https://github.com/third-intelligence/WARC</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <author>
      <name>Ku Onoda</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Haruto Kawano</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Shota Takashiro</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Kohei Hayashi</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.37570v1</id>
    <title>LCM-Promo: An LLM-Based Uplift Model for Promotion Allocation</title>
    <updated>2026-10-27T17:15:15Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.37570v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.37570v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>We present LCM-Promo, an LLM-based uplift model for promotion allocation. LCM-Promo is a task-specific Large Commerce Model (LCM): a finetuned LLM trained on transactional commerce data to predict user intent and promotion response. It learns monotonic promotion-response curves from production logs by interpreting single-token outputs as conversion-threshold probabilities, while conditioning on session context such as day and delivery shift. In a production A/B test on churn reactivation, LCM-Promo increased conversion by 1% and reduced promotion cost by 6% relative to the Segment-EMA production baseline.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.IR" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
