  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10193v1</id>
    <title>Ensemble Routing for Prompt-Adaptive Cost-Aware LLM Inference</title>
    <updated>2026-11-06T20:12:09Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10193v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.10193v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Large language models (LLMs) increasingly support Web-based services that must balance response quality and inference cost across heterogeneous requests. Conventional routing selects one model per prompt, while fixed ensembles cannot adapt their composition and cost to individual requests. We introduce PISA, a prompt-adaptive, cost-aware single-model router, and PISA-E, its extension to ensemble routing. At the core of both methods, PISA combines soft cluster memberships with model-specific predictors to estimate individual model capabilities. Building on these estimates, PISA-E jointly selects ensemble composition and cardinality under an explicit cost--quality preference that can be adjusted at inference time without retraining. A tractable ensemble-success surrogate and efficient candidate selection enable this extension without ensemble-specific training. Across four LLM routing benchmarks under in-distribution (ID) and out-of-distribution (OOD) conditions, PISA achieves the highest macro-average cost--performance hypervolume among the evaluated single-model routers in both settings. Meanwhile, PISA-E achieves the highest macro-average task performance among the evaluated ensemble approaches when prioritizing response quality, surpassing the strongest competitors by 2.09 and 2.87 percentage points under ID and OOD conditions, respectively. At selected lower-cost operating points, it also exceeds the strongest ensemble competitors' macro-average task performance while reducing average inference cost by approximately 36% and 25%, respectively. These results demonstrate the effectiveness of prompt-adaptive capability estimation for both single-model routing and selective ensembling.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-06T20:12:09Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Saul Urso</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Emanuele Carlini</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Patrizio Dazzi</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Matteo Mordacchini</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05927v1</id>
    <title>Modality-Aware Expert Pruning for MoE-Based Multimodal Large Language Models</title>
    <updated>2026-11-04T04:16:48Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05927v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.05927v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) enables multimodal large language models (MLLMs) to scale capacity efficiently, but expert parameters dominate memory even though only a few experts are activated per token: they account for over 90% of model size in modern MoE-MLLMs (e.g., 54 GB out of about 58 GB in BF16 for InternVL3.5-30B-A3B and Qwen3-VL-30B-A3B). Expert pruning has emerged as an effective compression strategy, but existing methods aggregate importance uniformly across tokens, ignoring that the same expert may serve different roles for vision versus language processing. To address this, we propose MAEP (Modality-Aware Expert Pruning), a training-free framework that decomposes expert importance by modality in a single forward pass. MAEP introduces (1) Delta-H Output ($ΔH$), a redistribution-aware metric measuring the MoE output change when an expert is removed, and (2) low-attention vision tokens as a vision-side pruning signal that is dataset-stable: its expert-importance ranking remains stable across different datasets. Text-side importance serves as a preservation signal while vision-side importance serves as a pruning signal, and experts are ranked globally across layers. Experiments on three MoE-based MLLMs across multimodal and text benchmarks show that MAEP outperforms modality-agnostic baselines on the majority of multimodal benchmarks while remaining competitive on text-only tasks, reducing the BF16 expert weight footprint by about 27 GB (about 47% of total memory) so that the full weight footprint of an approximately 58 GB model fits within a single 40 GB GPU. Code is available at https://github.com/nota-github/MAEP.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-04T04:16:48Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>NeurIPS 2026</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>Donggeun Kim</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07464v1</id>
    <title>FINCOSMOS: An Open-World Evaluation Paradigm for Agents in Finance</title>
    <updated>2026-11-05T05:19:44Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07464v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.07464v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Language agents increasingly work for users on the open Web, and finance is where trusting them is hardest. The data must come from the Web, the strategy lives in the user's head, and correctness depends on time, so an agent can fail silently: a strategy the user never asked for, unverified data, or a backtest that uses the future yields a report as convincing as a correct one. Existing benchmarks close the world somewhere, fixing the task, the data, or the timeline, and thereby remove these failures instead of measuring them. We introduce FINCOSMOS, an open-world evaluation paradigm for agents doing financial work. A simulated user holds a hidden strategy sampled from a compositional grammar and reveals it only when asked. The agent receives no data, so it must acquire data from the Web, build its own backtest under a data cutoff, and deliver verifiable results. Grading needs no reference answer. Programs verify point-in-time correctness and recompute every reported metric, the delivered strategy is checked against the hidden specification, and a run is accepted only when the user is satisfied and none of 15 veto checks fails. Across four coding agents and a model without tools in 150 sessions, no session is accepted: every session that the simulated user signed off fails a program check, and agents raise only 54--64\% of the details they were not told. Human experts attribute 88.33\% of failures to the agents. With FINCOSMOS feedback, an agent with persistent memory learned all ten tasks.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="q-fin.CP" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-05T05:19:44Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
