  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2512.23818v3</id>
    <title>Energy-Tweedie: Score meets Score, Energy meets Energy</title>
    <updated>2026-11-07T12:03:15Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.23818v3" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2512.23818v3" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Denoising and score estimation are classically linked through Tweedie's formula, which relates the posterior mean under Gaussian noise to the Stein score of the noisy marginal. In this work, we extend this perspective beyond Gaussian noise to a broad class of Gibbs (energy-based) noise distributions, with the generalized Gaussian family as an example. We derive the Energy-Tweedie identity: when the denoising posterior is viewed through the lens of scoring rules, the path derivative of a kernel scoring rule defined by the noise potential recovers the Stein score of the noisy marginal. Thus, the familiar correspondence between Gaussian noise, posterior means, squared loss, and Tweedie's formula is lifted to a distributional correspondence between Gibbs noise distributions, full posterior laws, kernel scoring rules, and the Energy-Tweedie identity, which gives a Tweedie-style relation for each noise potential. Among its consequences, this identity gives a posterior-samples-to-score route to score estimation, yields a principled criterion for estimating unknown noise parameters, and enables diffusion-style sampling along user-chosen paths through the noise-parameter space, supplying the score-based perspective on recent generative methods trained with scoring rules.</summary>
    <category term="stat.ML" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2025-12-29T19:28:50Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Code repository: https://github.com/andleb/et. NeurIPS 2026 Camera-ready, 59 pages, 16 figures</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="stat.ML"/>
    <author>
      <name>Andrej Leban</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10987v1</id>
    <title>Horizon-Scalable Q-Learning</title>
    <updated>2026-11-07T22:03:33Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10987v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.10987v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Learning accurate off-policy state-action value functions (Q-functions) from offline datasets becomes harder as the task horizon grows. One-step temporal-difference (TD) learning bootstraps after every transition, so small per-step errors accumulate along the horizon, and early states can be badly misvalued even when the TD loss is small. Multi-step TD shortens this chain by following the dataset trajectory for several steps; however, since the behavior policy is often suboptimal, every followed action adds a bias, and no fixed number of steps suits a whole trajectory. In this paper, we propose Horizon-Scalable Q-Learning (HQL), a simple method that enables accurate long-horizon value learning by automatically balancing between one-step and multi-step returns. HQL extends a target through a dataset action only when the critic values it at least as highly as the best-of-N actions sampled from the BC policy, and otherwise falls back to a one-step target. We show that this rule is a principled off-policy correction that needs no action densities, which makes HQL compatible with expressive flow and diffusion policies. Experiments show that HQL avoids the failures of both one-step and multi-step TD on a combination-lock task across horizons, and outperforms prior horizon-reduction methods on long-horizon OGBench and OCBench tasks.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-07T22:03:33Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <author>
      <name>Haoran Xu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Pragya Srivastava</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Xinjie Liu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Siddhant Agarwal</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Amy Zhang</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11890v1</id>
    <title>Training Vision Transformers to Focus: Emergence of Spatial Attention Head Specialization</title>
    <updated>2026-11-09T05:13:14Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11890v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.11890v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Self-supervised learning in Vision Transformer models, such as DINO, enables robust visual perception without requiring labeled data by leveraging large-scale image datasets. However, these models lack a focusing mechanism during training, resulting in perceptual representations that are often ambiguous or overly similar. In this work, we introduce two visual focusing loss functions designed to establish correspondence between the model's attention and specific regions within images. Specifically, we leverage the multi-head attention mechanism within the model to selectively steer the model's focus, leading to the emergence of spatial attention head specialization, characterized by diverse and spatially focused perceptual capabilities without requiring annotations. Through both qualitative and quantitative evaluations, we demonstrate that our method substantially increases the spatial selectivity and diversity of attention heads, improving both interpretability and performance on dense prediction tasks such as zero-shot image segmentation.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-09T05:13:14Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Accepted at NeurIPS 2026. Project page: https://yuji-nozawa.github.io/ReFoCo/</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>Yuji Nozawa</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yu-Chieh Lin</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Youyang Ng</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13425v1</id>
    <title>Prompt2Echo (P2Echo): Text-Prompted Multi-View Echocardiography Segmentation with One Model</title>
    <updated>2026-11-10T01:08:01Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13425v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.13425v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Automated segmentation in multi-view echocardiography is essential for comprehensive cardiac assessment, yet remains challenging due to substantial anatomical and appearance variability across standard acoustic windows. In this paper, we present P2Echo, a unified framework capable of segmenting diverse cardiac structures across multiple standard echocardiographic views using a single model controlled by structured view-aware text prompts. Unlike recent foundation model adaptations that rely on implicit latent priors, P2Echo explicitly conditions the segmentation process via a novel Dynamic Image-Text Alignment (DyITA) module. DyITA integrates a differentiable routing mechanism and token differential operators to dynamically modulate decoder features according to the requested view and target structure. This allows the network to adaptively switch its processing pathways for different views without architectural changes. Extensive experiments on a large-scale internal dataset (seen during training) and held-out evaluation on three external datasets demonstrate that P2Echo matches or exceeds both view-specific specialists and state-of-the-art universal models at a fraction of their computational cost, offering an efficient, controllable solution for multi-plane cardiac analysis. Code is available at: https://github.com/moeinheidari7829/P2Echo.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-10T01:08:01Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>10 pages, 5 figures, 7 tables</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
