  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13995v1</id>
    <title>Lipschitz-Controlled Flow Matching for Robust Image Restoration</title>
    <updated>2026-11-10T12:40:17Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13995v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.13995v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Flow-matching models can produce high-quality image restorations, but their learned dynamics may amplify small input perturbations and make reconstructions unstable. We introduce LipsFlower to reduce this amplification. We derive stability bounds for Flow-from-$Y$, which initializes the ordinary differential equation from a noise-perturbed observation, as well as for observation-conditioned posterior sampling, which conditions the velocity field on the observation throughout integration. Based on this analysis, we develop LipHDiT, a Lipschitz-aware hourglass diffusion transformer that serves as the vector-field architecture of LipsFlower. LipHDiT incorporates stabilized approximate spectral normalization, bounded modulation, and convex residual mixing. We also use adversarial finite-radius gain regularization at sampled interpolation states to penalize excessive sensitivity to the state and, for conditional sampling, the observation. We test both formulations on blind face restoration, measuring adversarial robustness and the perceptual quality of the reconstructions. LipsFlower reduces perturbation amplification while keeping restoration quality competitive with the evaluated baselines. These results suggest that Lipschitz-aware flow matching can improve the stability of generative image restoration.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-10T12:40:17Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>Sedjro Salomon Hotegni</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Konstantin Sonntag</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Paul-Christian Bürkner</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Sebastian Peitz</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14210v1</id>
    <title>Tree-Ising: Repairing Language Models with Bit-Level Ising Edits</title>
    <updated>2026-11-10T15:32:04Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14210v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.14210v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Weight-space programming, introduced in recent MIT work by Takehi et al., enables a frontier LLM agent to read a network's weights and write a repair. Their GPT-6 Astra agent corrected the reasoning loops of Qwen3.5-0.8B without gradients, using 158 agent turns, 89.7 million tokens, and 50 forward-pass requests. Our method, Tree-Ising, casts this repair as a sparse discrete optimization problem over the model's own BF16 weights. Forward statistics define an objective that weakens a loop direction, strengthens a concluding direction, and preserves the layer's output on productive tokens. Within a state-tree representation, where lossless BF16 storage acts as a special case, the admissible edits are sign flips and exponent shifts that preserve all shared group exponents and mantissas. We decompose this objective exactly into independent QUBO (Ising) problems, one per output row, which we solve on a GPU using simulated annealing or quantum-inspired simulated bifurcation. On the agent's two layers, Tree-Ising achieves 54.6% on MATH-500, outperforming the agent's edit (51.7%) under the same evaluation protocol, while the base model scores 7.9% as reported. The simulated bifurcation solver yields a statistically indistinguishable repair at 55.1%. Both solvers outperform the agent edit across all five math benchmarks, with statistically significant improvements on MATH-500 and GSM8K after correcting for multiple comparisons. Our approach modifies 13% to 17% fewer weights and reduces BF16 bit flips by a factor of 2.2 to 2.3. It requires only four forward-pass requests and zero LLM tokens, with the annealing recipe costing about $0.90 in GPU time. On Qwen3.5-2B, with layers and dose selected using only the base model, the same recipe increases greedy GSM8K accuracy from 21.6% to 69.4%. As a proof of concept, we execute an eight-variable repair subproblem using QAOA on an IBM Heron processor.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-10T15:32:04Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <author>
      <name>Hadi Salloum</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Ammar Ali</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
